module

public import WeilConjectures.Algebra.Lie.RestrictedEnvelopingAlgebra
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Defs
public import Mathlib.Data.List.FinRange
import WeilConjectures.Algebra.Lie.FieldFilteredPBW
import WeilConjectures.Algebra.Lie.AssociativeJacobsonIdentities

/-!
# Restricted PBW and presentation by basis relations

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Definition 10.39 and Theorem 10.40,
printed pp. 206–207 (blueprint: lem-sfb-restricted-pbw-basis).

The quotient is the tensor quotient by all defining relations. Ordered products use
`List.finRange`, retaining the order in the noncommutative algebra.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace RestrictedEnvelopingAlgebra

/-- The truncated ordered monomials form a basis, and basis bracket and p-relations
generate the full defining ideal. For an empty basis the sole monomial is one.
Source: Milne, Definition 10.39 and Theorem 10.40, printed pp. 206–207
(blueprint: lem-sfb-restricted-pbw-basis).

Proof plan from the node:
1. Bilinearity expands every tensor bracket relation in basis bracket relations.
   Pass to the ordinary enveloping algebra. Apply `lem-sfb-field-filtered-pbw`:
   ordered monomials form a basis, and those of degree at most `d` form the
   filtered basis. Concatenation adds the filtration degrees.
2. Put `c(x)=j(x)^p-j(q(x))`. The associative Jacobson sum identity
   (`lem-sfb-associative-jacobson-identities`) and the restricted sum axiom give
   additivity of `c`. The scalar axiom gives `c(ax)=a^p c(x)`.
   Consequently the basis values `c_i` generate all p-relations. The quotient
   by them is canonically the displayed tensor quotient, using the identity on tensors.
3. The adjoint Jacobson identity and restricted adjoint axiom give
   `[c(x),j(y)]=0`. The commutator product rule extends this to the algebra
   generated by the `j(y)`, so each `c_i` is central.
4. For `0 ≤ a_i < p` and `b_i ≥ 0`, expand `E^a` times the product of `c_i^b_i`.
   Its leading ordered word is `E^(a+pb)`. All other terms have degree at most
   `|a|+p|b|-1`, since `p ≥ 2`; ordering the leading word changes it only by
   lower degree brackets. Division with remainder identifies `(a,b)` with `a+pb`.
5. Degree induction shows that these mixed monomials span the ordinary enveloping
   algebra. A finite relation, in maximal degree, contradicts the independence of
   the filtered PBW monomials. Thus the mixed monomials form a basis.
6. Centrality implies that the ideal of the `c_i` is precisely the span of mixed
   monomials with some `b_i > 0`: multiplication by `c_i` increments that exponent,
   and every such monomial factors by a `c_i`. The complementary basis vectors
   with `b=0` therefore give the truncated monomial basis of the quotient.
7. In the tensor quotient by basis relations all brackets already hold, and step 2
   kills every p-relation. Both inclusions of defining ideals follow. If `n=0`,
   the scalar axiom gives `q(0)=0`, all relations vanish and the empty product one
   is the unique basis monomial. -/
theorem exists_restricted_pbw_basis
    (F : Type u) [Field F] (L : Type v) [LieRing L] [LieAlgebra F L]
    (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] [CharP F p] (q : L → L)
    (hq : LieAlgebra.IsRestrictedOperation F p q)
    (n : ℕ) (e : Module.Basis (Fin n) F L) :
    ∃ B : Module.Basis (Fin n → Fin p) F (RestrictedEnvelopingAlgebra F L p q),
      (∀ a : Fin n → Fin p,
        B a = ((List.finRange n).map (fun i ↦ ι F p q (e i) ^ (a i).val)).prod) ∧
      LieAlgebra.restrictedEnvelopingIdeal F p q =
        TwoSidedIdeal.span
          ({z | ∃ i j : Fin n, z = LieAlgebra.tensorBracketRelation F (e i) (e j)} ∪
            {z | ∃ i : Fin n, z = LieAlgebra.tensorRestrictedRelation F p q (e i)}) ∧
      (n = 0 → ∀ a : Fin n → Fin p, B a = 1) := by
  sorry

end RestrictedEnvelopingAlgebra
